    "evidence_text": "Robustly Leveraging Prior Knowledge in Text Classification\tPrior knowledge has been shown very useful to address many natural language processing tasks.\n\nIntroduction\tWe posses a wealth of prior knowledge about many natural language processing tasks.\n\nPrevious studies addressing the problem fall into several lines.\n\nFirst, to leverage prior knowledge to label data  ,  .\n\nSecond, to encode prior knowledge with a prior on parameters, which can be commonly seen\n\nin many Bayesian approaches  ,  .\n\nThird, to formalise prior knowledge with additional variables and dependencies  .\n\nWould the model be robust or sensitive to the prior knowledge?\n\nOr, which kind of knowledge is appropriate for the task?\n\nSuch prior knowledge may mislead the model with heavy bias to baseball.\n\nTo this end, we introduce auxiliary regularization terms in which our prior knowledge is formalized as distribution over output variables.\n\nRecall the example just mentioned, though we do not have enough\n\nWe survey related work in Section 4, and summarize our work in Section\n\nand performs well when we provide prior knowledge with no bias.\n\nSuch features are usually frequent words across all categories.\n\n$w_i$ given topic $t_j$ ).\n\nSimilar to  ,  , we estimate the reference distribution of the labeled features using a heuristic strategy.\n\nThe labeled features are obtained through information gain.\n\nThe original balanced movie dataset is used (positive:negative=1:1).\n\nthe balance among classes.\n\nRelated Work\tThere have been much work that incorporate prior knowledge into learning, and two related lines are surveyed here.\n\nThe other is to constrain the model directly with prior knowledge.\n\nChang et al.guiding proposed constraint driven learning.\n\na set of distributions that satisfy the auxiliary constraints.\n\nChen et al.chen2013leveraging tried to learn comprehensible topic models by leveraging multi-domain knowledge.\n\nThere are also some active learning methods trying to use prior knowledge.\n\nConclusion and Discussions\tThis paper investigates into the problem of how to leverage prior knowledge robustly in learning models.\n\nTo the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to address the robustness problem of leveraging knowledge, and may inspire other research.\n\nWe then present more detailed discussions about the three regularization methods.\n\nThe KL\n\ndivergence performs much better on unbalanced corpora than other methods.\n\nThe fact suggests that additional knowledge does benefit the model.\n\nHowever, the KL divergence term requires providing the true class distribution.\n\nOr, we can set the distribution roughly with domain expertise.",
